<content>
  <section><title>Cool Down Activity</title>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Now, combine your new understanding of factoring with using the <term data-schema-version="1.0" data-store="glossary-tooltip">zero product property</term> to solve equations.</para>
    <para>Solve the equation \(2x^2− 7x + 5 = 0\) by any method. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.</para>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the solution to the equation \(2x^2− 7x + 5 = 0\) and your explanation.</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para> \(x=\frac {5}{2}\) (or 2.5) and \(x=1\). Rewriting the expression in factored form gives \((2x − 5)(x − 1) = 0\). Using the zero product property: \(2x − 5 = 0\) or \(x − 1 = 0\), so \(x=\frac {5}{2}\) and \(x = 1\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<div class="os-raise-student-reflection">
  <para class="os-raise-student-reflection-title">Building Character: Curiosity</para>
  <div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-gap-1">
    <media><image src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b6a16c035326e4038afa935707124bbc204c7496"/>
    </media><div>
      <para class="os-raise-mb-0">People typically spend more time learning about things they are curious about and more readily remember the information. Curious people tend to be happier individuals. </para>
      <para>Think about your current sense of curiosity. Are the following statements are true for you?</para>
      <list>
        <item>
          When I didn’t know the answer to a question, I couldn’t rest until I figured it out.  
        </item>
        <item>
          I explored a completely new idea or topic—just for the fun of it. 
        </item>
      </list>
      <para>Don’t worry if none of these statements are true for you. Developing this trait takes time. Your first step starts today!</para>
     </div>
  </div>
</div></section>
</content>
